## A.1 `pro_memberships`

Mirror the shape of `provider_subscriptions` (`…20260705000000_scraped_claims_billing.sql`) so the two read consistently — same timestamp naming, same status vocabulary where it applies.

| column | type | notes |
|---|---|---|
| `id` | uuid | pk |
| `provider_id` | uuid | FK providers, not null |
| `status` | text + check | `active`, `lapsed`, `cancelled` — matches `provider_subscriptions`' vocabulary, minus `expired` |
| `source` | text + check | `purchased`, `package_included`, `granted` |
| `started_at` | timestamptz | not null |
| `current_period_end` | timestamptz | not null |
| `cancelled_at` | timestamptz | nullable |
| `price_paid` | numeric | nullable — null for `package_included` and `granted` |
| `yoco_ref` | text | nullable, follows the `yoco_ref` naming already established in `…20260804000000_yoco_payments.sql` |
| `notes` | text | nullable |
| `created_at` / `updated_at` | timestamptz | repo convention |

Partial unique index enforcing **one active row per provider**, exactly as `provider_subscriptions` does.

RLS following the pattern of `provider_subscriptions`: a provider reads their own row, writes are service-role only.

